  <details class="group bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200/80 shadow-sm overflow-hidden" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-black text-slate-900 text-base sm:text-lg select-none list-none group-hover:text-amber-600 transition-colors">
      <span itemprop="name">How do you distinguish between Western drywood and subterranean termites?</span>
      <span class="ml-4 flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-slate-100 flex items-center justify-center text-slate-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">
        <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
      </span>
    </summary>
    <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-slate-100 pt-4" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        Western drywood termites live entirely inside dry structural framing without requiring ground contact, ejecting characteristic six-sided hexagonal fecal pellets (frass) through kick-out holes. Western subterranean termites nest in damp soil and construct prominent mud tubes over foundation walls to safely access structural timber.
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="group bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200/80 shadow-sm overflow-hidden" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-black text-slate-900 text-base sm:text-lg select-none list-none group-hover:text-amber-600 transition-colors">
      <span itemprop="name">What safety protocols are followed for children and household pets?</span>
      <span class="ml-4 flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-slate-100 flex items-center justify-center text-slate-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">
        <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
      </span>
    </summary>
    <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-slate-100 pt-4" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        Field technicians adhere strictly to California Department of Pesticide Regulation (DPR) guidelines and EPA product labels. We prioritize non-repellent perimeter barrier applications, tamper-resistant rodent stations, and targeted gel baiting inside closed wall voids or cabinet hinges out of reach of children and family pets.
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="group bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200/80 shadow-sm overflow-hidden" itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-black text-slate-900 text-base sm:text-lg select-none list-none group-hover:text-amber-600 transition-colors">
      <span itemprop="name">Are one-time treatments sufficient, or is recurring maintenance necessary?</span>
      <span class="ml-4 flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-slate-100 flex items-center justify-center text-slate-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">
        <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
      </span>
    </summary>
    <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-slate-100 pt-4" itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        While acute issues like isolated wasp nests or localized flea outbreaks can often be addressed in a single targeted visit, persistent coastal species such as Argentine ants, spiders, and roof rats benefit significantly from recurring quarterly or bi-monthly perimeter defense to maintain continuous barrier strength.
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>

